Scott's Exercise 6.29 asks for the infinitary separated sum \(\sum_{n=0}^{\infty} D_n\) and product \(\prod_{n=0}^{\infty} D_n\), and whether \(\oplus\) and \(\otimes\) admit the same generalization. The development uses an arbitrary index family. `iprod` is the product whose neighbourhoods are the cylinders `iprodNbhd` of finitely supported tuples, where `FinSupp` is the positive list condition that every index off the list equals the master of its factor; `iprodEquiv` is the order isomorphism between product elements and the pointwise product of the factor elements, with `proj` reading a neighbourhood \(U\) at index \(i\) as membership of the slice `slice` \(i\) \(U\) and `fromPi` assembling a tuple of elements. `proj_fromPi` recovers the \(i\)-th component because `fromPi_mem_slice` says the assembled element contains that slice exactly when the component contains \(U\). `fromPi_toPi` recovers the element: a cylinder in the reassembly lies in every projection, so every slice lies in the original element, and `z_mem_of_slices` rebuilds the cylinder from a support list by `z_mem_iprodNbhd_restrictTo`, whose cons step is `iprodNbhd_restrictTo_cons` (the restricted cylinder is the head slice intersected with the tail restriction) and whose empty step is the product master. `isum` is the separated sum on optional tagged tokens, with neighbourhoods the sum master `sumMasterI` and a single tagged copy `injI`; `isum_summand_unique` says an element that meets tagged copies at two indices meets them at the same index, because the element contains their intersection, that intersection is not the master (the basepoint lies in `sumMasterI` by `none_mem_sumMasterI` and in no tagged copy by `none_not_mem_injI`), and a token of the resulting tagged copy forces the indices together by `index_of_some_mem_injI`; `isum_trichotomy` splits on the existence of some tagged neighbourhood in the element and, in the other case, concludes that every neighbourhood is the master. `ioplus` is that sum with the tagged copies restricted to neighbourhoods distinct from the summand master, so the coalesced sum is defined at every index. `iotimes` requires a proper neighbourhood to be non-master at every coordinate and still finitely supported; `iotimes_only_master` shows that over an infinite index the support list would contain every index, by double negation from the positive support law, and `iotimes_subsingleton` concludes there is at most one element because every neighbourhood equals the master and every element contains the master. `example_629` conjoins only `iotimes_only_master`, `iotimes_subsingleton`, `isum_summand_unique`, and `isum_trichotomy`, and its gloss repeats those four claims. The `FinSupp` gloss states that the list contains every index at which the tuple fails to be the master, whereas the definition is \(\exists l,\ \forall i,\ i \notin l \to X_i = \mathrm{master}\), the form used when `FinSupp.inter` concatenates supports. **Theorem:** partial — `iprod` with `iprodEquiv`, `isum`, and `ioplus` are the infinitary product, separated sum, and coalesced sum, and `iotimes_only_master` with `iotimes_subsingleton` refutes an infinite smash, while `example_629` and its gloss contain only the smash collapse and the two separated-sum element lemmas. **Proof:** partial — those four packaged arguments match their glosses, and the product round-trip is the slice reconstruction above, while the proof of `example_629` is the conjunction of the four lemmas and includes neither `iprodEquiv` nor any property of `ioplus`.
